      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "**Solution approach**\n\nUnattended customers cost 10000 each, so the solver must serve all 10 customers. Vehicles cost 1000 each, and lateness and waiting cost 10 per minute, which is heavy relative to distance. The time-window structure drives the route design:\n\n- Customer 9 (due at 35, service 40) must be the first stop of its route. Starting from the depot, it finishes at 60.12 and cannot reach 3, 4, or 7 in time.\n- Customers 7 and 4 (due at 64 and 69) cannot both be served on time in one route. Visiting 7 first gives a lateness of about 20.5 minutes at customer 4.\n- Customer 3 (due at 83) is best served alone. Any insertion into another route adds lateness of 100+ minutes.\n- Customer 1 (due at 99) must be the first stop of its route to be served on time, and 5 can follow it with only a small delay.\n- The late-deadline customers 2, 8, 6, and 10 can absorb their travel and service times within their wide windows, except for small lateness at 2 and 10.\n\nI compared several groupings. Those that combined 5 with 6 and 10 incurred large lateness, and inserting 3 into any other route was clearly worse. The best grouping found uses four vehicles:\n\n1. **Route 1:** 0 → 9 → 8 → 2 → 0. Distance 223.98, lateness 0.78 at customer 2 (cost 7.75).\n2. **Route 2:** 0 → 3 → 0. Distance 94.02, no waiting or lateness.\n3. **Route 3:** 0 → 7 → 4 → 6 → 10 → 0. Distance 272.99, lateness 20.49 at customer 4 and 11.77 at customer 10 (cost 322.6).\n4. **Route 4:** 0 → 1 → 5 → 0. Distance 197.76, no waiting or lateness.\n\n**Score**\n\n- Unattended customers: 0\n- Vehicles: 4 × 1000 = 4000\n- Accumulated distance: 223.98 + 94.02 + 272.99 + 197.76 = 788.75\n- Waiting time: 0\n- Late arrival: 0.78 + 20.49 + 11.77 = 33.04, times 10 = 330.4\n\nTotal ≈ 4000 + 788.75 + 330.4 ≈ 5119.1\n\nThis is a heuristic result from a structured enumeration of the main alternatives, not a proof of optimality.\n\n{\"routes\": [[9,8,2],[3],[7,4,6,10],[1,5]], \"score\": 5119.1}",
